:: ds_lecture_d3
<div class="intimate"><span class="tag">The dream, made real</span>
<<img "kavya_cs_lecture.png" "Room C-204">>
Room C-204. Tiered seating. A wide clean whiteboard. The smell of dry-erase markers, which   unlike formalin   makes this borrowed heart sing.

You sit in Arjun's seat, third row from the back, left, near the window. Rohit drops in beside you. "Bhai, tera face glow kar raha hai. Kya kha ke aaya?"

"Chai." But the glow is real. Arjun's face is smiling and you can't stop it.

Prof. Krishnan enters   mid-fifties, sharp eyes, the posture of a man who treats every lecture like a conversation with colleagues. He writes: <b>Red-Black Tree Balancing   Deletion Cases.</b>

Your brain ignites. The neural pathways that have starved on phone screens and stolen library sessions light up like a grid after a blackout. The six deletion cases. Double-black resolution. Sibling analysis. Rotation sequences. You've solved these   on paper during pharmacology, on LeetCode at 2 AM, on the back of an anatomy worksheet during Dr. Sharma's spot quiz.

You sit through the whole ninety minutes with your hand twitching in your lap, a mind that knows the answer and has spent three years forbidden from raising its hand.

<<run setup.flag("in_lecture_d3")>>
<<run setup.flag("cs_lecture_attended_d3")>>
