:: kavya_evening_coding_d3
<div class="intimate"><span class="tag">Intimate marker · first erection</span>
<<img "kavya_coding_joy.png" "PG room, 5:35 PM">>
Rohit's gone. Door locked. VS Code open. The flow state arrives in ninety seconds   a record. On the Redmi it takes ten minutes of buffering and frustration.

You create the project: <code>ml-medical-imaging</code>. A CNN to classify chest X-rays. Data pipeline first. Then the model   ResNet backbone, custom head, dropout for regularisation. The code pours. Function after function. Arjun's fingers dance and the mechanical switches click and the sound is a percussion section playing to the rhythm of creation.

And then, around 5:35, something else happens.

You become aware of a sensation between your legs. A warmth. A thickening. The penis   soft, resting, unremarkable for twelve hours   is changing. Growing. Not suddenly; gradually, like a slow boot sequence, blood filling tissue in increments. You feel each one. It lifts from its resting position and presses, insistently, against the inside of the jeans.

There's a bulge. An actual, visible, denim-straining bulge where thirty seconds ago there was none.

This is not arousal. You're thinking about learning-rate schedulers. The erection is //random//   the male body's equivalent of a pop-up ad, an unsolicited notification from the genital subsystem, arriving without trigger or permission. You shift in the chair and the friction of denim against the sensitised head sends a bright jolt through your pelvis and you //gasp//   Arjun's voice, gasping, in an empty room.

You sit very still. Think about Cockcroft-Gault equations. The Krebs cycle. Dr. Mhatre droning through pharmacokinetics. Aggressively boring things.

Four minutes. The blood retreats, the tissue softens, the penis returns to rest against your thigh.

//This happens to boys randomly? Regularly? Without warning? How do they attend lectures? How do they ride motorcycles?//

You file it under //male biology, subcategory: design flaws// and keep coding. By 7:30 the project has a functional training pipeline, evaluation metrics, and documentation. More clean deployable code in one afternoon than three months on a phone. Your eyes sting. One tear   Arjun's single, bureaucratic tear   escapes.
</div>

